Gaming desktop PCs are classified as automatic data processing machines in heading 8471. They are presented as a complete system in a single housing, which falls under subheading 8471.30 only if the unit is portable and weighs no more than 10 kg. A desktop is not portable, so it goes to 8471.49 or 8471.50. Heading 8471.50 covers processing units other than those of 8471.41 or 8471.49. A gaming PC with only a processing unit and no keyboard or monitor would go there.

The description says only that the PC is a traditional desktop and mentions no monitor or peripherals in the shipment. So 8471.50 is the best fit. The 10-digit statistical suffix under 8471.50 is 8471.50.0150 for processing units for digital processing machines, "other" (the 8471.50.0150 line is "Other" under "Digital processing units..."). I recall 8471.50.01 as "Digital processing units other than those of subheadings 8471.41 and 8471.49, whether or not entered with the rest of a system," with the 10-digit codes 8471.50.0150 ("Other") and 8471.50.0140 ("For automatic data processing machines of subheading 8471.30"). CBP rulings on gaming PCs generally cite 8471.50.0150.

{"hts_code": "8471.50.0150", "reason": "Desktop gaming PCs are non-portable digital processing units of heading 8471, which falls under 8471.50 (other digital processing units), with 0150 as the statistical suffix for 'other'."}
